  "news": [
    {
      "tag": "Macro",
      "title": "Two stories, one tape: AI caution and a pipeline attack",
      "summary": "The day had a single dominant thread with two ends. Leaders of the biggest AI companies publicly called for slowing down frontier model development, and a Saudi oil pipeline was shut after a drone attack. The first knocked down chipmakers and the AI buildout trade; the second pushed oil sharply higher and dragged the 10-year Treasury yield toward 5% on renewed inflation worry. Both landed two days before the Fed's rate decision, which is what gave them their weight."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Stocks",
      "title": "AI leaders call for a slower pace of development",
      "summary": "Anthropic's Dario Amodei and OpenAI's Sam Altman both highlighted the safety risks of how fast AI models are advancing. There's no rule or filing behind it, just a position taken out loud, but the market read it straight through to the money that builds AI: Nvidia, Micron and the rest of the chip complex sold off, and the Nasdaq and semiconductor indexes lagged. Cybersecurity names went the other way."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Macro",
      "title": "Saudi Arabia shuts its East-West oil pipeline after a drone attack",
      "summary": "The line that lets crude bypass the Strait of Hormuz was taken offline, which removes the workaround exactly when the strait itself is the worry. Brent pushed toward $105-110 and WTI followed. Higher oil feeds directly into inflation expectations, so the bond market moved too \u2014 the 10-year yield briefly topped 5% and expectations for another Fed hike firmed."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Macro",
      "title": "The 10-year yield touched a multi-year high near 5%",
      "summary": "Bonds sold off as oil-driven inflation fears built through the session, lifting the 10-year Treasury yield to its highest in years just ahead of Wednesday's Fed decision. When yields move like this two days before a rate announcement, it's the market pre-positioning for what it thinks the Fed will say."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Stocks",
      "title": "Indexes closed lower, but most stocks didn't",
      "summary": "The S&P 500 and Nasdaq both finished modestly down under the weight of AI and oil, yet the majority of individual stocks actually rose. That gap is worth noticing: the headline indexes are dominated by a handful of very large companies, so when those few are the ones being sold, the average stock can have a perfectly decent day while the number on the screen is red. Software and defensive areas did the offsetting."
    },
    {
      "tag": "Stocks",
      "title": "Chips led the losses while cybersecurity rallied hard",
      "summary": "Nvidia, Broadcom, Micron and the ETFs tracking them took the brunt of the selling. CrowdStrike and its peers ran the opposite direction on the view that a world more cautious about AI spends more on protecting against it, not less \u2014 the same headline read as a cost by one group and as demand by another."
    }
  ],
